  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 123 AM EST Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Low level moisture trapped beneath an inversion \nhas led to low clouds over the Adirondacks overnight. We also \nhave some fog forming in the valleys where the skies have \ncleared as surface high pressure is in control of our weather \nwith light winds. Center of surface high pressure will remain \nnearly stationary to the southwest of our region, but this will \nprovide us with very quiet weather through this week. \nTemperatures will be about 10 degrees above seasonal normals \nduring the day, and about 5 degrees above normal at night. Each \nday we should have partly to mostly sunny skies, with mainly \nclear nights as well.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Our nice clear and dry stretch will hold on for \none more day this weekend on Saturday before shower chances \nreturn for Sunday into Monday. Temperatures Saturday will be \nsimilar to that of the work week with highs in the mid to upper \n50s and generally clear skies as a secondary ridge, associated \nwith our overarching high passes through. The high centered over\nthe Mid-Atlantic will slide east, but models remain uncertain \nas to how much the high departs, in addition to how much it \nweakens. This will play a role for our shower chances Sunday \ninto Monday. There is strong model agreement that a coastal low \nwill develop off the Carolina coast Sunday. However, models \nbegin to diverge on the track of the system towards next week. \nThe GFS and notably the Google AI Deepmind phase this system \nwith an approaching Great Lakes shortwave late Sunday bringing \nwidespread shower chances from south to north; the steadiest of \nwhich is focused over southern Vermont. This track depicts the \ndeparting high as keeping its strength off the east coast, \namplifying the Great Lakes shortwave, and dragging the coastal \nlow north into our region. Conversely, the ECMWF and CMC weaken \nthe departing high, keeping our pattern more progressive, and \ntracking the coastal low out to see, more near Benchmark. This \nleaves us with a weaker Great Lakes shortwave, which favors \nlighter showers mainly across the Adirondacks and areas near the\nInternational Border. Currently consensus favors the latter of \nthe two solutions, but we will closely monitor trends for the \nweekend into next week. Regardless, our temperatures will return\nto seasonable levels to start off next week with highs back \ninto the 40s.",
